<description>
Swap the two foreground colors inside each separated 3x3 object.

Input structure:
- The grid has a dominant background color.
- Every foreground object is a solid 3x3 rectangle connected by 4-connectivity.
- Each object contains exactly two non-background colors.

Rule:
- Preserve the background and object positions.
- Within each object, replace every pixel of either foreground color with the
  other foreground color from the same object.

Key insight:
- The transformation is local to each object: the two colors in a 3x3 motif
  are complements that exchange roles, independent of the colors used by
  other objects.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "4-connected solid 3x3 foreground objects, each with exactly two colors, on a background",
  "transform": "within each object, swap its two foreground colors while preserving the background and object positions"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 7-13 rows x 7-12 cols, background color 0
  objects:
    count: 1-4 solid 3x3 rectangles (all 9 cells non-background)
    colors: each object uses exactly 2 distinct non-background colors
    color_split: minority 2-4 cells, majority 5-7 cells (observed splits (4,5), (3,6), (2,7))
    color_sharing: colors may be reused across objects [observed]
    no_symmetry_requirement: patches may or may not have LR/UD symmetry
  placement:
    border_padding: >=1 cell from every grid edge
    separation: min L-inf distance >=2 between any two objects (no touching, not even diagonally)
    all_foreground_pixels: every non-bg cell belongs to exactly one 3x3 object
  transform:
    type: transform_in_place
    rule: within each 3x3 object, swap its two foreground colors (a->b, b->a); background unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (simple enough that output-first is unnecessary)
  steps:
    1. Choose grid dimensions in range 7-13 rows, 7-12 cols
    2. Choose number of objects N in 1-4
    3. For each object, pick 2 distinct non-bg colors and a split (minority_count in 2-4)
    4. Fill a 3x3 patch randomly with the two colors matching the chosen split
    5. Place objects on the grid with >=1 border padding and >=2 L-inf separation between all pairs
    6. Output is obtained by swapping the two colors within each object
```
</generation_spec>
